\vspace{10pt}

    Lessons from Midway: Shaping a Thriving Future from the Past 

\vspace{10pt}

    By the turn of the 21* century, the American flag has become a symbol of global security. 

\vspace{10pt}

    Like many others of my generation, I am fortunate to have only ever known a world of freedom, 

\vspace{10pt}

    peace, and prosperity. Yet, we must remember that these everyday rights were hard-won—earned 

\vspace{10pt}

    through the blood, sweat, and tears of American soldiers in World War II. The Battle of Midway, 

\vspace{10pt}

    a pivotal moment in the Pacific Theater, stands as a powerful reminder of that sacrifice. By 

\vspace{10pt}

    looking to the past, we can learn valuable lessons to build a thriving future. 

\vspace{10pt}

    In the 1930s, a new imperial power arose in the East: The Empire of Japan. Determined to 

\vspace{10pt}

    free Asia from European influence, Japan established the Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere 

\vspace{10pt}

    [1]. Sensing Japan as a growing threat, the U.S. declared an oil embargo in 1941, cutting off 80\% 

\vspace{10pt}

    of Japan’s oil supply [2]. However, instead of deterring Japan, this prompted the Japanese to 

\vspace{10pt}

    seize more Allied territories for resources. Admiral Yamamoto, commander-in-chief of the 

\vspace{10pt}

    Japanese Combined Fleet, believed that in order to continue its expansion, Japan had to 

\vspace{10pt}

    neutralize the U.S. Thus, in December 1941, Japanese aircraft attacked the U.S. Pacific Fleet at 

\vspace{10pt}

    Pearl Harbor, Hawaii [2]. The next day, the United States declared war on Japan. As Yamamoto 

\vspace{10pt}

    remarked, “I fear all we have done is to awaken a sleeping giant and fill him with a terrible 

\vspace{10pt}

\begin{figure}[h]

\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{/convert/w7gAXc1/localstore/essay-contest-the-battle-of-midwayassets/1.jpg}

\centering

\end{figure}

    1942 was a year with heightened tensions across the Pacific Theater. In May, Japanese and 

\vspace{10pt}

    USS. fleets clashed for the first time at the Battle of Coral Sea. From the beginning, it was clear 

\vspace{10pt}

    that the Japanese fleet outmatched the American fleet in terms of both quality and quantity. 

\vspace{10pt}

    Nonetheless, it was the strategic thinking, decision-making, and resilience of Americans at the 

\vspace{10pt}

    Battle of Midway that claimed the ultimate victory. 

\vspace{10pt}

\par

\vspace{10pt}

\begin{figure}[h]

\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{/convert/w7gAXc1/localstore/essay-contest-the-battle-of-midwayassets/2.jpg}

\centering

\end{figure}

    Participant ID: 13MA45103   Lessons from Midway: Shaping a Thriving Future from the Past  27 April 2025   Word Count: 1456 words 

\vspace{10pt}

    Strategy was key in the Battle of Midway. Located halfway between Japan and Hawaii, 

\vspace{10pt}

    Midway Atoll held strategic value as a U.S. naval base. Yamamoto believed claiming the island 

\vspace{10pt}

    would give the Japanese an upper hand in continuing its conquest of the Pacific and entice 

\vspace{10pt}

    American carriers into battle. His plan was to finish what Pearl Harbor started by destroying the 

\vspace{10pt}

    remaining U.S. fleet in a surprise ambush. But, unbeknownst to the Japanese, U.S. Naval 

\vspace{10pt}

    Intelligence at Pearl Harbor had already discovered their plan. 

\vspace{10pt}

    American cryptanalysts had decoded the Japanese naval code JN25. Through translating 

\vspace{10pt}

    radio interceptions, they hypothesized that a planned attack on a location nicknamed “AF” could 

\vspace{10pt}

    be referring to Midway. To confirm this, they ingeniously fooled the Japanese by sending out an 

\vspace{10pt}

    unencrypted message from Midway stating that the island was “running out of fresh water”. 

\vspace{10pt}

    Soon after, the Japanese reported that “AF” was running out of water, confirming to U.S. 

\vspace{10pt}

    intelligence that “AF” indeed meant Midway [3]. These findings gave Americans a tremendous 

\vspace{10pt}

    advantage in preparation. By doing this, the U.S. had already won half the battle. 

\vspace{10pt}

    The Battle of Midway also shows how flexibility in decision-making can determine the 

\vspace{10pt}

    outcome of a conflict. As Sun Tzu said, “Military tactics are like water.” This means that military 

\vspace{10pt}

    decisions must flow and adapt according to changing surroundings. A good general must be 

\vspace{10pt}

    prepared for any unexpected situation. Early in the morning of June 3, 1942, the Japanese fleet 

\vspace{10pt}

    was approaching Midway when Admiral Yamamoto was notified of unanticipated American 

\vspace{10pt}

    carriers in the area. Even so, Yamamoto maintained radio silence and refrained from informing 

\vspace{10pt}

    Admiral Nagumo, who oversaw the main carrier battle group [4]. Because of this poor 

\vspace{10pt}

    communication, Nagumo would go on to follow the original plan despite the completely changed 

\vspace{10pt}

