\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{/convert/w7gAXc1/localstore/essay-contest-the-battle-of-midwayassets/3.jpg}

\centering

\end{figure}

\par

\vspace{10pt}

\begin{figure}[h]

\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{/convert/w7gAXc1/localstore/essay-contest-the-battle-of-midwayassets/4.jpg}

\centering

\end{figure}

    Participant ID: 13MA45103   Lessons from Midway: Shaping a Thriving Future from the Past  27 April 2025   Word Count: 1456 words 

\vspace{10pt}

    On June 4, 108 Japanese planes launched a successful first strike on Midway [3], but a 

\vspace{10pt}

    second strike was needed to finish off the island’s defenses [4]. At 7:15 am, Nagumo ordered all 

\vspace{10pt}

    his planes to rearm from torpedoes to land bombs in preparation. However, just ten minutes later, 

\vspace{10pt}

    a scout reported sighting ten American ships. In this moment, Nagumo had to choose to continue 

\vspace{10pt}

    the second strike on Midway or confront the American ships. At 7:45 am, Nagumo ordered all 

\vspace{10pt}

    planes still armed with torpedoes to prepare for attacking the American fleet. This decision 

\vspace{10pt}

    allowed time for refueling returning planes and launching a coordinated attack—but it also 

\vspace{10pt}

    delayed action, leaving the Japanese carriers vulnerable. In this situation, Nagumo could have 

\vspace{10pt}

    retreated his fleet to buy more time for reequipping his planes. But, the cultural value of honor 

\vspace{10pt}

    may have hindered Nagumo from making the safest, most rational choice. Furthermore, obliging 

\vspace{10pt}

    to follow Yamamoto’s original plan prevented Nagumo from being a flexible leader. 

\vspace{10pt}

    While inflexibility troubled Japanese leadership, American commanders were willing to 

\vspace{10pt}

    adapt to unfolding realities. Mid-morning on June 4, Commander McClusky and his air group 

\vspace{10pt}

    arrived at the predicted location of the Japanese fleet only to find that the Japanese had already 

\vspace{10pt}

    changed course. Instead of returning to the USS Enterprise, McClusky decided to continue 

\vspace{10pt}

    northwest, where his group finally located the Japanese destroyer Arashi. This decision 

\vspace{10pt}

    demonstrates the courage and determination of American soldiers. If McClusky hadn’t been 

\vspace{10pt}

    flexible enough to alter his plans, perhaps the U.S. may have never located the Japanese fleet. It 

\vspace{10pt}

    was one commander’s valiant intuition that brought forth the most consequential hour at the 

\vspace{10pt}

    Battle of Midway. At 10:22 am, McClusky’s Dauntlesses crippled both the carriers Kaga and 

\vspace{10pt}

    Akagi, while 17 more dive-bombers from the USS Yorktown struck the Soryu. Within five 

\vspace{10pt}

    minutes, three out of the four Japanese carriers were up in flames [5]. The key decisions made by 

\vspace{10pt}

\par

\vspace{10pt}

