\vspace{10pt}

    Admirals Yamamoto, Nagumo, and McClusky exemplify how flexibility and communication in 

\vspace{10pt}

    leadership can greatly impact any situation. 

\vspace{10pt}

    The Japanese possessed an advantage in terms of technology, but the Americans were 

\vspace{10pt}

    unbeatable in their resilience. At noon on June 4, dive-bombers from the only remaining 

\vspace{10pt}

    Japanese carrier, Hiryu, badly damaged the USS Yorktown’s flight deck. At 2:30 pm, Hiryu's 

\vspace{10pt}

    torpedo planes struck the Yorktown's port side, causing it to tilt. At 5:00 pm, Captain Buckmaster 

\vspace{10pt}

    ordered all servicemen to “abandon ship” [4]. A veteran from the USS Yorktown remembered, “I 

\vspace{10pt}

    got sick and swam away from the ship for three or four hours” [6]. Photographer Bill Roy 

\vspace{10pt}

    described it as “the most difficult thing...you’re in the water and you can’t breathe... you’re 

\vspace{10pt}

    throwing up or getting sick... it burns your eyes, burns your nose... you’re gulping seawater and 

\vspace{10pt}

\begin{figure}[h]

\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{/convert/w7gAXc1/localstore/essay-contest-the-battle-of-midwayassets/6.jpg}

\centering

\end{figure}

    On June 6, 149 brave men along with 26 officers volunteered as a salvage team to save 

\vspace{10pt}

    the beloved USS Yorktown. Destroyer USS Hammann accompanied the wounded carrier, offering 

\vspace{10pt}

    dewatering pumps and keeping watch for Japanese attacks. But hope became heartbreak at 1:35 

\vspace{10pt}

    pm, when the USS Hammann was fatally struck by a Japanese submarine. Just two hours later, 

\vspace{10pt}

    two more torpedoes tore into the Yorktown’s underside. On June 7, 1942, USS Yorktown sank, 

\vspace{10pt}

    bringing along with it 141 honorable servicemen. [5]. Nevertheless, the Yorktown left behind a 

\vspace{10pt}

    legacy of hope, courage, and unwavering determination. Incredible resilience was shown by both 

\vspace{10pt}

    the crew of the Yorktown as they helped one another and braved the dangerous seawaters. 

\vspace{10pt}

    Remaining hopeful in the hardest moments was what allowed the soldiers to persevere to safety. 

\vspace{10pt}

    The final victory at Midway was earned by the American soldiers who fought until the very end. 

\vspace{10pt}

\par

\vspace{10pt}

\begin{figure}[h]

\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{/convert/w7gAXc1/localstore/essay-contest-the-battle-of-midwayassets/7.jpg}

\centering

\end{figure}

    Participant ID: 13MA45103   Lessons from Midway: Shaping a Thriving Future from the Past  27 April 2025   Word Count: 1456 words 

\vspace{10pt}

    During the Battle of Midway, U.S. intelligence was vigilant and proactive, often 

\vspace{10pt}

    outthinking the Japanese. Moreover, both sides faced difficult decisions, but it was the American 

\vspace{10pt}

    leadership’s flexibility under pressure that tipped the scale. Finally, American soldiers never gave 

\vspace{10pt}

    up in the face of a more advanced opposition. To this day, thousands of soldiers remain at the 

\vspace{10pt}

    bottom of the Pacific Ocean, eternally defending Midway. Their sacrifice turned the tide of the 

\vspace{10pt}

    Pacific Theater, halting momentum in Japanese expansion in favor of the Allies. After a 

\vspace{10pt}

    humiliating loss, the pride of Japanese generals was turned into alarm and caution. Conversely, 

\vspace{10pt}

    the U.S. would go on to triumph in a series of battles in its island-hopping campaign. 

\vspace{10pt}

    More than eight decades later, the Battle of Midway still teaches the importance of 

\vspace{10pt}

    strategy, flexibility, and resilience. Without these three qualities, the Allies may have never 

\vspace{10pt}

    secured victory and the age of equality, freedom, and peace that followed. As we face our own 

\vspace{10pt}

    battles today—whether personal, societal, or global—we must remember Midway. Success 

\vspace{10pt}

    begins with a well-crafted strategy, yet the future is unpredictable. It calls for the agility to adapt, 

\vspace{10pt}

    the strength to persist, and the vision to lead. Our world now rests in the hands of a new 

\vspace{10pt}

    generation. And like those who came before, it is our duty to rise, to lead with wisdom, and to 

\vspace{10pt}

    shape a future defined not by conflict, but by justice, liberty, and harmony. 

\vspace{10pt}

\par

